
  Two constraints I found that will shape it: [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is a plain browser script with no module system or bundler
  ([38;5;153mindex.html:13[39m loads it via [38;5;153m<script src>[39m), while [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m and [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m use CommonJS and aren't loaded by the
  page at all. So "shared across the app" has to bridge two worlds that currently don't talk. There's also no test
  runner or dependencies in [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m.

  First question — it determines the rest of the design:

  The fork is what the identifier actually [3mis[0m. A value that survives logout and identifies a browser is a fundamentally
  different thing from one the server issues after it verifies a password, and they lead to different storage, different
  lifetimes, and different privacy obligations.

  - [1mAnonymous visitor ID[0m — created client-side on first visit, persists indefinitely, identical before and after login.
    Buys funnel analytics across the whole app, works today with no backend. Costs: it identifies a browser, not a
    person; clearing storage resets it; in the EU/UK, persistent identifiers for analytics generally need consent.
  - [1mPer-session ID[0m — regenerated each visit, dies with the tab or browser. Buys clean single-visit tracing with the
    lightest privacy footprint. Costs: you can't correlate a user's visits over time, which is usually the thing people
    want from "track who logged in."
  - [1mAuthenticated user ID[0m — issued by the server when it verifies credentials, stored after login. Actually answers "who
    logged in." Costs: [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is a stub that never calls [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m, so this needs a real auth response contract
    first, and it's absent until someone logs in — other forms on public pages would have nothing.
  - [1mBoth layers[0m — an anonymous ID always present, upgraded/linked to the real user ID once authenticated. This is what
    mature analytics does. Costs the most: two identifiers, a linking step, and reconciliation rules.

  Hard to change later: the choice between anonymous and authenticated, because it determines whether pre-login forms
  can use the value at all. Cheap to change: storage mechanism and key names.

  I'd recommend the [1manonymous visitor ID[0m — it's the only one that works across every form today without inventing a
  backend contract, and it leaves room to link a real user ID later if you go that way.

